  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x)=2({3}^{x-5})-3.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ then $a=$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943819/assets/3e2c3036fb3f8c92ab09067fca4117e6fead9026e2fd926e174fefbe9037cbcf.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=-3$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -3\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3.$",
